### Why does a shared queue cause starvation?

If OTP, security alerts, and marketing all flow through one topic and one consumer group, messages are processed in arrival order. When a campaign enqueues tens of millions of messages, those millions sit ahead of the next OTP. Even if consumers are fast, the sheer backlog means an OTP waits behind millions of messages, and a delayed OTP is a failed login.

```java
package com.bank.notification.antipattern;

// WRONG: single shared topic for all priorities
public class SharedTopicAntipattern {
    // Topic: "notifications"
    // Consumer group: "notification-workers"
    // Message order: [MARKETING, MARKETING, ...(10 million)..., OTP, MARKETING, ...]
    // OTP at position 10,000,001 waits for 10 million marketing messages to drain.
    // OTP delivery latency: seconds -> minutes. Login fails.
}
```

### How does physical isolation fix this?

Create separate topics for critical (OTP, security) and bulk (marketing), each with its own consumer group.

```java
package com.bank.notification.config;

public class TopicConfig {

    public static final String CRITICAL_TOPIC = "notifications-critical";
    public static final String BULK_TOPIC = "notifications-bulk";

    // Critical consumer group: small, steady, always-on fleet
    public static final String CRITICAL_GROUP = "critical-consumers";

    // Bulk consumer group: large, elastic fleet
    public static final String BULK_GROUP = "bulk-consumers";

    // The two groups have independent offsets, independent lag,
    // and independent scaling. Critical is never affected by bulk.
}
```

### How do you classify and route at the edge?

Classify each notification by type and route to the correct topic at enqueue time.

```java
package com.bank.notification.router;

public class NotificationRouter {

    private final KafkaProducer producer;

    public NotificationRouter(KafkaProducer producer) {
        this.producer = producer;
    }

    public void route(Notification notification) {
        String topic;
        switch (notification.getType()) {
            case OTP:
            case SECURITY_ALERT:
            case FRAUD_ALERT:
                topic = TopicConfig.CRITICAL_TOPIC;
                break;
            case MARKETING:
            case PROMOTIONAL:
                topic = TopicConfig.BULK_TOPIC;
                break;
            default:
                topic = TopicConfig.BULK_TOPIC; // safe default
        }
        producer.send(topic, notification.getUserId(), notification);
    }
}
```

### Why must provider capacity also be isolated?

If both pipelines share one provider rate limit, a marketing blast consumes the entire rate budget and the critical pipeline is starved at the final hop even with separate queues.

```java
package com.bank.notification.provider;

public class IsolatedProviderRate {

    // WRONG: shared rate limit
    // TokenBucket shared = new TokenBucket(10_000); // both pipelines share

    // CORRECT: reserved rate for critical
    private final TokenBucket criticalBucket = new TokenBucket(2_000); // guaranteed
    private final TokenBucket bulkBucket = new TokenBucket(8_000);     // remainder

    public boolean trySendCritical() {
        return criticalBucket.tryConsume();
    }

    public boolean trySendBulk() {
        return bulkBucket.tryConsume();
    }
}
```

### Why is a single priority queue weaker?

A priority queue helps but still shares consumers, provider rate, and failure modes. A poison message or provider throttle triggered by marketing volume can still affect OTP.

| Aspect | Single Priority Queue | Physical Isolation |
|---|---|---|
| Consumer threads | Shared | Independent per pipeline |
| Provider rate | Shared | Reserved per pipeline |
| Failure domain | Shared | Independent |
| Pause marketing | Cannot pause without pausing OTP | Pause bulk, keep critical |
| Independent scaling | No | Yes |

```java
package com.bank.notification.antipattern;

// WRONG: single queue with priority flag
// A poison message in the shared consumer crashes it.
// Marketing volume triggers provider throttle -> OTP also throttled.
// Cannot pause marketing without pausing OTP.

// CORRECT: physical isolation (see above)
```

### How do you pause bulk during incidents?

The bulk consumer group can be paused, throttled, or stopped while the critical group keeps running untouched.

```java
package com.bank.notification.ops;

public class PipelineControl {

    public void pauseMarketing() {
        // Pause bulk consumer group: stop consuming from bulk topic
        // Critical consumer group continues unaffected
        System.out.println("Marketing pipeline PAUSED. OTP pipeline running.");
    }

    public void resumeMarketing() {
        System.out.println("Marketing pipeline RESUMED.");
    }
}
```

### How do you monitor each pipeline?

Track critical-path latency and lag as a strict SLO with tight alerts. Bulk lag is expected to be large during campaigns.

```java
package com.bank.notification.monitoring;

public class PipelineMonitor {

    public void checkLatency(String pipeline, long lagMs) {
        if ("critical".equals(pipeline) && lagMs > 5000) {
            // ALERT: OTP latency above 5 seconds!
            System.err.println("CRITICAL ALERT: OTP lag = " + lagMs + "ms");
        }
        if ("bulk".equals(pipeline) && lagMs > 3600_000) {
            // Warning: marketing more than 1 hour behind
            System.out.println("BULK WARNING: marketing lag = " + lagMs + "ms");
        }
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

How would you add a third priority tier for transactional alerts between critical and bulk? How do you handle a notification that was classified as marketing but is actually time-sensitive? What happens if the critical pipeline itself becomes overloaded? How do you share cost metrics between the two pipelines?
